"use strict";(self.webpackChunkguardian_public=self.webpackChunkguardian_public||[]).push([["6607"],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"guardian-progressing-with-monsson-and-hyperfy","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/guardian-progressing-with-monsson-and-hyperfy","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ratio1/GUARDIAN-ELIAS-pub/tree/main/blog/2026-11-03-progress-with-monsson-and-hyperfy.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-03-progress-with-monsson-and-hyperfy.md","title":"GUARDIAN progresses with MONSSON and Hyperfy","description":"GUARDIAN is progressing with both validation partners \u2014 SLM-based second-opinion agents for energy-sector operations with MONSSON, and efficient SLM deployment on CPU-first edge and VPS infrastructure in a physical-security co-pilot demonstrator with Hyperfy (GOC).","date":"2026-11-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Announcement","permalink":"/blog/tags/announcement","description":"Public announcements about the GUARDIAN project"}],"readingTime":2.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"GUARDIAN team","title":"Naeural SRL (Ratio1)","url":"https://ratio1.ai","page":null,"key":"guardian-team"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"guardian-progressing-with-monsson-and-hyperfy","title":"GUARDIAN progresses with MONSSON and Hyperfy","description":"GUARDIAN is progressing with both validation partners \u2014 SLM-based second-opinion agents for energy-sector operations with MONSSON, and efficient SLM deployment on CPU-first edge and VPS infrastructure in a physical-security co-pilot demonstrator with Hyperfy (GOC).","authors":["guardian-team"],"tags":["announcement"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Sustainable Edge AI on Local CPUs","permalink":"/blog/sustainable-edge-ai-on-local-cpus"}},"content":"*Acknowledgement: The GUARDIAN project received financial support through the [ELIAS 2nd FSTP Open Call](https://elias-ai.eu/opportunities/elias-2nd-open-call/). ELIAS \u2014 the European Lighthouse of AI for Sustainability \u2014 is funded by the European Union\'s Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under grant agreement No. 101120237.*\\n\\nGUARDIAN is progressing with both of its validation partners:\\n[MONSSON](https://www.monsson.eu/) in the energy sector and\\n[Hyperfy](https://hyperfy.tech/) (GOC) in physical security. Both strands\\napply the same idea: smaller models, including small language models\\n(SLMs), running on modest, CPU-first infrastructure close to the data,\\nwith a human operator making every decision.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nGUARDIAN started on 1 July 2026, and its six-month implementation runs to\\n31 December 2026. The project is building a toolkit and a benchmark\\nprotocol for running operational AI on commodity edge nodes and low-end\\nvirtual private servers (VPS), and for measuring what that takes in\\nlatency, data transfer and resource use.\\n\\n## Energy sector: second-opinion agents with MONSSON\\n\\nWith MONSSON, GUARDIAN is working on SLM-based second-opinion agents for\\nenergy-sector operations centers, in renewable-storage and\\nvirtual-power-plant monitoring scenarios. Such an agent is designed to\\nreview monitoring alerts and give the operator a second opinion: what it\\nnoticed, why, and how uncertain it is. The operator decides what, if anything, to do. Each\\nagent is a workload under the GUARDIAN benchmark, to be measured for\\nlatency, data locality and resource footprint across low-power edge,\\nlow-end VPS, local-server and central/cloud profiles. This is GUARDIAN\'s\\nprimary validation route. More on the\\n[energy-sector use case](/docs/use-cases/energy-monitoring).\\n\\n## Physical security: efficient SLM deployment in a co-pilot demonstrator with Hyperfy (GOC)\\n\\nWith Hyperfy (GOC), GUARDIAN is working on efficient SLM deployment \u2014\\nrunning small language models on CPU-first edge and VPS infrastructure \u2014\\nto be demonstrated in one bounded operations-center co-pilot scenario for\\nphysical security. This is a demonstrator, not a deployment in a\\npartner\'s live operations center. Candidate tasks include alarm\\nclustering, false-alarm review and event summarization. More on the\\n[operations-center use case](/docs/use-cases/operations-center-copilot).\\n\\n## Boundaries\\n\\nBoth scenarios are advisory-only: operators stay in control, and GUARDIAN\\nperforms no live asset control, automated enforcement, biometric\\nidentification or live dispatch. The work uses approved partner, derived,\\nanonymized or synthetic data, and no raw operational data or security\\nmedia are published.\\n\\n*Update, 20 November 2026: one set of examples from the physical-security\\npilot has since been published with written permission: 19 cropped camera\\nframes of alerts tagged as false alarms, the\\n[GUARDIAN Second Opinion Dataset 1, public subset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/ratio1/guardian_second_opinion_ds_1_public).*\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nThe validation work feeds two reports planned for November 2026 \u2014 the\\nprimary validation report with MONSSON and the operations-center co-pilot\\ndemonstrator report with Hyperfy \u2014 and the final report, public summary,\\ndemo video and handover package planned for December 2026. See\\n[Deliverables](/docs/project/deliverables) for the full list. Measured\\nresults will be published on [Results & Downloads](/docs/results) once\\nthey are available and approved for release."},{"id":"sustainable-edge-ai-on-local-cpus","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/sustainable-edge-ai-on-local-cpus","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ratio1/GUARDIAN-ELIAS-pub/tree/main/blog/2026-08-04-sustainable-edge-ai-on-local-cpus.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-04-sustainable-edge-ai-on-local-cpus.md","title":"Sustainable Edge AI on Local CPUs","description":"Acknowledgement: The GUARDIAN project received financial support through the ELIAS 2nd FSTP Open Call. ELIAS \u2014 the European Lighthouse of AI for Sustainability \u2014 is funded by the European Union\'s Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under grant agreement No. 101120237.","date":"2026-08-04T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Article","permalink":"/blog/tags/article","description":"Project articles and technical write-ups"}],"readingTime":7.93,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"GUARDIAN team","title":"Naeural SRL (Ratio1)","url":"https://ratio1.ai","page":null,"key":"guardian-team"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"sustainable-edge-ai-on-local-cpus","title":"Sustainable Edge AI on Local CPUs","authors":["guardian-team"],"tags":["article"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"GUARDIAN progresses with MONSSON and Hyperfy","permalink":"/blog/guardian-progressing-with-monsson-and-hyperfy"},"nextItem":{"title":"ELIAS 2nd Open Call evaluation result","permalink":"/blog/naeural-srl-ranked-2nd-elias-2nd-open-call"}},"content":"*Acknowledgement: The GUARDIAN project received financial support through the [ELIAS 2nd FSTP Open Call](https://elias-ai.eu/opportunities/elias-2nd-open-call/). ELIAS \u2014 the European Lighthouse of AI for Sustainability \u2014 is funded by the European Union\'s Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under grant agreement No. 101120237.*\\n\\nGenerating a single image with a modern AI model takes about as much energy as fully charging your smartphone. Furthermore, if you extrapolate that out to the sheer volume of prompts, generations, and automated tasks happening globally every single second, it paints a pretty terrifying picture: we are looking at these sprawling, hyper-cooled server farms out in the desert.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThey\'re consuming the electrical equivalent of entire mid-sized nations. The wild thing is that the tech industry has essentially accepted this as a given, treating the massive carbon footprint as basically an unavoidable tax on progress. We\'ve decided that artificial intelligence is inherently this giant, centralized, power-hungry machine\u2014a total brute-force approach to innovation. It\'s like we took the mindset of the Industrial Revolution and slapped it onto data processing: you want a smarter model, just build a bigger data center, buy massive compute clusters, and pump more gigawatts of power into the cooling systems. The whole architecture is completely addicted to centralized scale.\\n\\n>... It\'s like we took the mindset of the Industrial Revolution and slapped it onto data processing: you want a smarter model, just build a bigger data center...\\n\\n## Introducing Ratio1 GUARDIAN\\n\\nThe project stands for Green, User-guided, Auditable Resource-aware Deployment for Industrial AI Networks, a dense acronym that serves as a perfect roadmap for what the project is trying to achieve technically.\\n\\nOur ELIAS-based project proposed a six-month, 10 person-month sprint to deliver a reusable toolkit and benchmark specifically designed to run operational AI on commodity edge nodes and low-end virtual private servers, or VPSs. This is an architectural rebellion: a shift away from GPUs. We are so used to the paradigm that running AI requires massive GPU clusters. The whole industry is obsessed with buying every available GPU to train and run these monolithic models, but GUARDIAN proposed something completely countercultural\u2014CPU-first, smaller-model inference.\\n\\nThe mechanical differences between the hardware explain why this is such a big deal. The AI industry shifted heavily to GPUs around 2012 because of their architecture. A GPU is essentially thousands of tiny, relatively weak processing cores that execute simple mathematical operations simultaneously. This massive parallelization is perfect for the heavy matrix multiplication required to train deep neural networks, but the trade-off is massive: GPUs consume a staggering amount of electricity, require intense thermal cooling, and are incredibly expensive to purchase and operate.\\n\\nBy contrast, a CPU, or central processing unit, does not have thousands of cores; it might have 8, 16, or 32 highly complex, incredibly fast cores. If a GPU is an army of 10,000 middle schoolers doing basic arithmetic all at once, a CPU is a room full of 10 genius-level professors solving complex sequential logic puzzles one at a time, incredibly fast. CPUs are everywhere\u2014in every standard server, every laptop, and every piece of industrial equipment. They are cheap, and their power consumption is a fraction of that of a GPU array.\\n\\nGUARDIAN is essentially betting that specific localized AI tasks, such as anomaly detection in an industrial setting, do not need an army of 10,000 middle schoolers in a centralized cloud. You can use the professors already sitting in the server rack next to the machinery, running a smaller, highly focused model directly on the local CPU and completely bypassing the massive energy tax of cloud computing.\\n\\nWhat made the GUARDIAN proposal so compelling to the [ELIAS](https://elias-ai.eu/) evaluators was its restraint: the documents are extremely careful to state explicitly that we are not making wild, unverified carbon-reduction claims.\\n\\nInstead of making grandiose claims, GUARDIAN focuses strictly on reporting resource-footprint proxies. In practice, we are building a methodology to measure data locality, meaning trying to mathematically prove how much energy is saved by keeping data on the local factory floor rather than transmitting gigabytes of telemetry data across the internet to a centralized server. The transmission alone costs energy. GUARDIAN also measures execution latency and serving patterns on these low-power edge nodes, building a localized evidence base against which sustainability hypotheses can be tested.\\n\\nThe leverage is our existing technology, and our proposal made it clear that we are using the proprietary Ratio1 platform as the foundation.\\n\\nA bounded monitoring workload is an AI system given a very strict, highly localized task, such as watching time-series data from a specific industrial system for anomalies that indicate something is about to break or go wrong. The critical psychological barrier in high-stakes environments, such as managing a national energy grid or securing a physical facility, is fear of autonomous action. Enterprise buyers are fundamentally terrified of black-box AI. They do not want an algorithm making an unexplainable decision that automatically shuts down a power plant or locks a corporate headquarters without human intervention; they want what the documents call an advisory-only system.\\n\\nThey want a system that raises its hand and says: \\"I have noticed a 14% anomalous drop in voltage on turbine number four over the last 20 minutes. Based on my local model, I am 87% confident this represents a failing capacitor. Here is the specific data stream that led me to this conclusion. You, the human operator, need to look at this.\\"\\n\\nIt is the fundamental difference between hiring an autonomous CEO and hiring a diligent intern. GUARDIAN is essentially selling the enterprise market the ultimate AI intern: it stays awake 24 hours a day, seven days a week, tirelessly reviewing mountains of incredibly boring time-series data. It flags anomalies and, crucially, leaves a meticulous, mathematically sound audit trail of its work right on your desk, but it never makes the final executive decision.\\n\\nThat auditability is the entire ball game for enterprise adoption. By providing clear confidence signals, uncertainty metrics, and a transparent audit trail, GUARDIAN aims to address the trust issue, which its planned validation is designed to test. From a purely strategic go-to-market perspective, this creates an incredibly low-friction commercial route for our startup.\\n\\nAchieving TRL 5 validation gives GUARDIAN the exact evidence class that its broader commercial strategy, our Ratio1 RedMesh flagship product (https://redmesh.ai), is currently only touching. In that sales cycle, a corporate customer does not have to authorize a multi-million-dollar overhaul of its entire IT infrastructure, upload all its highly sensitive proprietary data to a centralized cloud controlled by a tech giant, or surrender operational control to a machine.\\n\\nInstead, the customer can begin with a bounded technical assessment, buy a cheap localized CPU server, install the GUARDIAN edge software or a modular piece of its framework, and run the AI intern in parallel with existing systems. It can inspect the evidence pack, review the audit trail, and slowly build organizational trust in the technology before committing to a full deployment. It is a brilliant Trojan horse strategy for getting AI into hesitant legacy industries.\\n\\nOur proposal outlined two specific contexts for validating this highly efficient, auditable, edge-based anomaly-detection toolkit. The primary focus is the energy sector, where GUARDIAN is partnering with [MONSSON](https://www.monsson.eu/) to monitor renewable-energy storage and virtual power plants. The secondary pilot is an operations-center co-piloting demonstrator focused on physical-security workflows, partnered with a company called [Hyperfy](https://hyperfy.tech/) or GOC. Energy grids and physical security are therefore its initial proving grounds.\\n\\n## Conclusion\\n\\nThis leaves a massive structural question about where all of this is heading. If this architecture works, if GUARDIAN proves that highly complex, highly accurate anomaly detection can run on cheap localized CPUs without a massive data center, it completely upends the accepted narrative of the tech industry, with profound implications for the future of operational infrastructure. \\n\\n> The GUARDIAN proposal highlighted an intentional shift away from the centralized cloud and back to the localized edge, as a hypothesis that its planned measurements are designed to test.\\n\\nIf this highly auditable, tiny-model approach succeeds at scale and becomes the standard for how enterprises adopt AI in daily operations, are we looking at the beginning of the end of the cloud era for specialized artificial intelligence? What happens to the balance of power in the global technology industry when every manufacturing plant, every hospital, every local server rack, and every corporate operations center can run its own completely private, highly sustainable AI\u2014one that requires a fraction of the electricity and never has to communicate with a tech giant\'s centralized desert server farm?\\n\\nIt decentralizes the entire ecosystem, strips leverage from massive cloud providers, and puts the power of machine learning directly back into the hands of local operators, with absolute privacy and a potentially lower carbon footprint, a hypothesis that GUARDIAN\'s planned measurements are designed to test rather than a demonstrated result. It is a phenomenal counter-narrative to everything we are told about the future of this technology. The massive hyper-cooled server farm might not be the inevitable endgame after all. \\n\\n> The future of daily operational AI might be a quiet, highly efficient CPU sitting in the corner of your office, diligently auditing your systems, keeping your data completely private, and leaving a paper trail for you to review in the morning."},{"id":"naeural-srl-ranked-2nd-elias-2nd-open-call","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/naeural-srl-ranked-2nd-elias-2nd-open-call","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ratio1/GUARDIAN-ELIAS-pub/tree/main/blog/2026-07-14-elias-2nd-open-call-evaluation-result.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-14-elias-2nd-open-call-evaluation-result.mdx","title":"ELIAS 2nd Open Call evaluation result","description":"ELIAS has published the ranking list of its 2nd Open Call, in which the GUARDIAN proposal by Naeural SRL (Ratio1) ranked 2nd.","date":"2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Announcement","permalink":"/blog/tags/announcement","description":"Public announcements about the GUARDIAN project"}],"readingTime":0.36,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"GUARDIAN team","title":"Naeural SRL (Ratio1)","url":"https://ratio1.ai","page":null,"key":"guardian-team"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"naeural-srl-ranked-2nd-elias-2nd-open-call","title":"ELIAS 2nd Open Call evaluation result","description":"ELIAS has published the ranking list of its 2nd Open Call, in which the GUARDIAN proposal by Naeural SRL (Ratio1) ranked 2nd.","authors":["guardian-team"],"tags":["announcement"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Sustainable Edge AI on Local CPUs","permalink":"/blog/sustainable-edge-ai-on-local-cpus"}},"content":"import FundingStatus from \'@site/src/components/FundingStatus\';\\n\\nELIAS has published the\\n[official ranking list](https://elias-ai.eu/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/ELIAS-2nd-Open-Call-Ranking-List.pdf)\\nof its 2nd Open Call.\\n\\n<FundingStatus />\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nMore about GUARDIAN is on the [About page](/docs/about) and the\\n[Evaluation Result](/docs/elias/evaluation-result) page.\\n\\nMore information on the ELIAS project and its open calls is available at\\n[elias-ai.eu](https://elias-ai.eu)."}]}}')}}]);